+- `cargo test --test spec_runbook_drill` — [the drill](../tests/support/drill.rs) in
+  `tests/support/` — replays the outage twice: the server comes back (nothing is lost) and the server
+  never comes back (the drill reports the loss). Both have to hold; the second one is what makes the
+  first one evidence.
+- `deliveries_total{outcome="given_up"}` back at zero, and `POST /signup` still at 200 for a fresh
+  address.
+- a fresh signup's message arrives at the mail server within the retry budget — the queue, not the
+  request path, is what was broken.
 
 ## Escalation
 
-- [what to do when the mail server is not coming back today](TODO)
-- [who or what to involve, and with what information](TODO)
+- **the mail server is not coming back today**: the queue is bounded
+  ([`MAX_ATTEMPTS`](../src/delivery.rs) is three attempts), so mail *will* be given up on; the honest
+  move is to stop accepting signups (a deployment decision, and `GET /readyz` is the lever an
+  orchestrator has) and to say so.
+- **the database is the thing that is away**: this page does not apply; start with `GET /readyz`
+  ([why it exists](../src/http.rs)) and the migration order of `mailer/1.9-postgres`.
+- **given-up mail keeps climbing after the server is back**: a queue that is retrying into a server
+  which is up is a bug on our side, not an outage — [the worker](../src/delivery.rs) and the
+  `MAILER__EMAIL__…` settings are where to look, and the drill is what to reproduce it with.
